TypeScript is a programming language developed and maintained by Microsoft. It is a typed superset of JavaScript that adds optional static typing, class-based object-oriented programming, and mode-specific compiler checks. TypeScript's type system helps catch errors and makes large-scale JavaScript applications more maintainable by reducing the occurrence of runtime errors.

Duck Typing, also known as Structural Typing, is a concept often used in dynamically typed programming languages, including JavaScript (in its run-time form). In Duck Typing, a variable or parameter is expected to behave in a certain way, not necessarily by having a specific type assigned to it during compilation. Instead, the variable or parameter's compatibility with the expected behavior is determined by its runtime properties and methods. If an object has the necessary properties and methods that a piece of code is expecting, it can be used in the same way as if it had been specifically declared as that type.

In TypeScript, although it is a statically typed language, Duck Typing is also supported, particularly through the use of interfaces. An interface in TypeScript is a construct that defines a set of properties and methods that a class or a variable must implement or possess in order to be compatible with that interface. When a variable or a parameter is declared as of a specific interface type, TypeScript's compiler will verify that the object satisfies the interface's requirements at compile time. If the object has the necessary properties and methods, it will be considered as being of that interface type.

For example, here's an interface definition in TypeScript:
```
interface Shape {
  area(): number;
}
```
A variable or a class that implements this Shape interface must have an area() method that returns a number. Now, we can define different classes that implement this interface:

```
class Square implements Shape {
  constructor(private sideLength: number) {}
  area() {
    return this.sideLength * this.sideLength;
  }
}

class Circle implements Shape {
  constructor(private radius: number) {}
  area() {
    return Math.PI * Math.pow(this.radius, 2);
  }
}
```

In this example, both Square and Circle classes have the required area() method, which makes them compatible with and being able to implement the Shape interface. Typescript will ensure that all variables or parameters that require a Shape entity will work with either a Square or a Circle object as long as the object has the necessary area() method, which satisfies the interface requirements.

In summary, both TypeScript and Duck Typing (in this context, represented through interfaces) provide a way to ensure types and implement type-checking and compatibility, but they differ in how types are defined and enforced. In TypeScript, types are generally defined at compile-time with static typing, while Duck Typing, like in JavaScript, focuses more on runtime behavior and object structure.

To create a Discord.js v14 bot with a single slash command called "ban," follow these steps:

1. Install the required dependencies:
   a. Open a terminal or command prompt in your project directory and run `npm install discord.js` to install Discord.js library.
   b. Install the required package for slash commands using `npm install @discordjs/builders @discordjs/rest @discordjs/typedia` to access the necessary builders and utilities.
2. Create a new file named `index.js` and import the required modules:
   ```javascript
   const { Client, GatewayIntentBits, Partials } = require('discord.js');
   const { REST } = require('@discordjs/rest');
   const { Routes } = require('discord-routes');
   const { v4: uuidv4 } = require('uuid');
   ```
3. Initialize the Discord.js client:
   ```javascript
   const client = new Client({
     intents: [
       GatewayIntentBits.Guilds,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildMessages,
       GatewayIntentBits.GuildPresences,
       GatewayIntentBits.MessageContent,
     ],
     partials: [Partials.Message, Partials.Channel, Partials.Reaction],
   });
   ```
4. Define the slash command for the "ban" command:
   ```javascript
   const banCommand = {
     name: 'ban',
     description: 'Bans a member from the server.',
     options: [
       {
         name: 'member',
         description: 'The member to ban.',
         type: 7,
         required: true,
       },
       {
         name: 'reason',
         description: 'The reason for the ban.',
         type: 3,
         required: false,
       },
     ],
     defaultPermission: true,
   };
   ```
5. Set up the REST client for interacting with the Discord API:
   ```javascript
   const REST = new REST({ version: '9' }).setToken('YOURBotDiscordTOKEN');
   ```
   Replace `YOURBotDiscordTOKEN` with your actual Discord bot token.
6. Register the ban command:
   ```javascript
   const registerCommands = async () => {
     try {
       // Register global application commands
       const applicationCommands = [banCommand];
       const globalCommands = applicationCommands.filter(
         (command) => !command.defaultPermission,
       );

       await REST.put(
         Routes.applicationGuildCommands('YOUR_CLIENT_ID', 'YOUR_GUILD_ID'),
         { body: globalCommands },
       );

       await REST.put(
         Routes.applicationCommands('YOUR_CLIENT_ID'),
         { body: applicationCommands },
       );

       console.log('Successfully registered application commands!');
     } catch (error) {
       console.error(error);
     }
   };

   client.once('ready', () => {
     console.log('Bot is ready!');
     registerCommands();
   });

   client.login('YOURBotDiscordTOKEN');
   ```
   Replace `YOUR_CLIENT_ID` with your Discord bot client ID, which can be found in the Discord Developer Portal. Also replace `YOUR_GUILD_ID` with the ID of the server where you want to register the ban command. You can find the server ID by clicking on the server information and copying the number after the `id = ` parameter.

7. Implement the ban command logic in the client:
   ```javascript
   client.on('interactionCreate', async (interaction) => {
     if (!interaction.isCommand()) return;

     switch (interaction.commandName) {
       case 'ban':
         const member = interaction.options.getMember('member');
         const reason = interaction.options.getString('reason');

         if (!member || member.user.id === client.user.id) {
           // Return if member not found or if the bot tries to ban itself
           return interaction.reply({
             content: "I couldn't find the specified member or the bot can't ban itself.",
             ephemeral: true,
           });
         }

         try {
           // Ban the member
           await interaction.guild.members.ban(member, {
             days: 7,
             reason: `${interaction.user.tag} (${interaction.user.id}) - ${reason}`,
           });

           // Send a confirmation message
           await interaction.reply({
             content: `${member.user.tag} has been successfully banned.`,
             ephemeral: false,
           });
         } catch (error) {
           // Handle any errors encountered during the ban process
           console.error(error);

           await interaction.reply({
             content:
               "An error occurred while attempting to ban the member. Please try again later.",
             ephemeral: true,
           });
         }
         break;
       default:
         break;
     }
   });
   ```
This code defines a command object with the name "ban," description, and options - member and reason (optional). It then registers the command locally and globally using the REST API. The logic for the "ban" command is implemented by checking if the member exists and handling any errors that may arise during the banning process.

Here is a C++ function that converts markdown formatted text to HTML using the std::regex and std::string manipulation functions:
```c++
#include <string>
#include <regex>

std::string ConvertMarkdownToHTML(const std::string& markdown) {
  // Define regular expression patterns for markdown syntax
  std::regex italicsRegex("\\*(.*)\\*");       // Italic Text (asterisk enclosed)
  std::regex boldRegex("\*\*(.*)\*\*");        // Bold Text (double asterisk enclosed)
  std::regex headersRegex("\\n# (.+)");       // Headers (using # followed by a word)
  std::regex linksRegex("\\[([^\]]+)\\]\\(([^)]+)\\)"); // Links [(text)](url)
  std::regex imagesRegex("![([^\]]+)]\\(([^)]+)\\)");  // Images ![alt text](url)
  std::regex listItemsRegex("\\t (\\d*. )+");     // List items (with tab and leading number or letter, followed by space)
  std::regex blockquotesRegex("\\>(.*)");         // Blockquotes (> followed by content)

  // Use std::regex_replace to convert matched markdown syntax to HTML
  std::string output = markdown;
  output = std::regex_replace(output, italicsRegex, "<i>\\1</i>");    // Convert italics to HTML
  output = std::regex_replace(output, boldRegex, "<b>\\1</b>");      // Convert bold to HTML
  output = std::regex_replace(output, headersRegex, "<h6>\\1</h6>"); // Convert headers to HTML (h6 for markdown style)
  output = std::regex_replace(output, linksRegex, "<a href=\"\\2\">\\1</a>"); // Convert links to HTML
  output = std::regex_replace(output, imagesRegex, "<img src=\"\\2\" alt=\"\\1\">"); // Convert images to HTML
  output = std::regex_replace(output, listItemsRegex, "<li>\\1<\\li>", std::regex_constants::format_first_only); // Convert list items to HTML
  output = std::regex_replace(output, blockquotesRegex, "<blockquote>\\1<\\/blockquote>"); // Convert blockquotes to HTML

  return output;
}
```
This function uses std::regex to match and replace markdown syntax patterns in the input string (`markdown`) with the respective HTML equivalents. The function calls std::regex_replace for each pattern, with proper capturing groups to insert matched text as necessary. For example, italics are enclosed by `<i>...</i>`, bold is enclosed by `<b>...</b>`, headers are converted to `<h6>...</h6>`, links and images are converted to `<a href="...">...</a>` and `<img src="..." alt="...">`, and list items are turned into `<li>...</li>` tags. Blockquotes are converted to HTML blocks. Note that this function assumes that line breaks in markdown are represented with `\n` in the input string.

