Here's a Java solution to determine if an integer n is a power of three without using loops or recursion:

```java
class PowerOfThree {
    public boolean isPowerOfThree(int n) {
        // If n is negative, it can't be a power of three
        if (n < 0) return false;
        // The largest power of 3 that can be represented by an int is 3^19
        if (n > Integer.MAX_VALUE / 3) return false;
        // Using the fact that 3^(m+n) = 3^m * 3^n, we can repeatedly divide by 3 until we get a result less than 3
        while (n >= 3) {
            if (n % 3 != 0) return false;
            n /= 3;
        }
        return n == 1; // The remaining value must be 1 to make n a power of 3
    }
}
```

This solution works by first checking if n is negative, which would make it impossible to be a power of three (returns false). Then, it checks if n is greater than the largest power of 3 that can be represented by an `int`, which is 3^19, and returns false if it is. Next, it repeatedly divides n by 3 using the fact that 3^(m+n) = 3^m * 3^n, and it stops dividing when n becomes less than 3. At this point, if the remaining value n is not 1, it indicates that n was not a power of 3, so false is returned. If n is equal to 1, it means n is a power of three (3^1), and true is returned.

