Developing a C++ program to count the number of occurrences of a specific word in a text file line by line can be achieved using the following steps:

1. Include necessary headers:
   ```
   #include <fstream>  // For file input/output
   #include <iostream> // For standard input/output
   #include <string>   // For working with strings
   ```
2. Define a function to count word occurrences:
   ```
   int countWordOccurrences(const std::string& word, const std::string& inputText) {
      int count = 0;
      size_t position = inputText.find(word);
      while (position != std::string::npos) {
         count++;
         position = inputText.find(word, position + word.length());
      }
      return count;
   }
   ```
   This function takes in the word to be counted (`word`) and the input text (`inputText`) as parameters, then counts the number of occurrences by searching for the word from the beginning of the text (`position = inputText.find(word)`). The loop continues until the word is not found anymore (`position == std::string::npos`).

3. Implement the main function:
   ```
   int main() {
      // Replace "filename.txt" with the actual file name
      std::ifstream inputFile("filename.txt");
      if (!inputFile) {
         std::cerr << "Error opening file!" << std::endl;
         return -1;
      }

      std::string line;
      std::string wordToCount; // Specify the word to be counted

      int wordCount = 0;
      while (std::getline(inputFile, line)) {
         wordCount += countWordOccurrences(wordToCount, line);
      }
      inputFile.close();

      std::cout << "Number of occurrences of '" << wordToCount << "' in the file: " << wordCount << std::endl;

      return 0;
   }
   ```
   The main function opens a file named "filename.txt" for reading and counts the occurrences of the specified word, `wordToCount`, in each line of the file. The total word count is accumulated by summing up the occurrences from each line before closing the file.

